# Hourly Chime toggled ON, captured from hardware 2026-07-27.
# Diff against a clean baseline where every config key was 0/false.
#
# Exactly two keys moved:
#   key 66: 0 -> 1
#   key 77: [0, 21600, 64800] -> [1, 21600, 64800]
#
# Key 77 is therefore [enabled, start_seconds, end_seconds] for the chime,
# and the times match the 06:00 / 18:00 limits shown in the app. An earlier
# guess that key 77 was sunrise/sunset was wrong: those times were defaults.
# Stored as decoded values rather than hex, since these are CBOR arrays.
# key66_before: 0
# key66_after: 1
# key77_before: [0, 21600, 64800]
# key77_after: [1, 21600, 64800]
#
# Follow-up: the chime was then disabled and sunrise/sunset enabled instead.
#   key 77: [1, 21600, 64800] -> [0, 21600, 64800]   (chime enable, both ways)
#   key 66: 1 -> 1                                    (unchanged)
#
# Two conclusions:
#  1. key 66 is not chime-specific, since disabling the chime left it at 1.
#     Consistent with a count of enabled config features.
#  2. Sunrise/sunset produced NO visible change whatsoever. The app took a
#     location and computed real times, and none of them appear in any of the
#     22 readable properties. Portland's actual sunrise/sunset (~05:50, ~20:45)
#     are absent; only the round 06:00/18:00 chime defaults are present.
#     That config lives in the 6 properties the truncated read never returns.
#
# Final round: sunrise/sunset disabled, returning every config feature to off.
#   key 66: 1 -> 1   PREDICTION WAS 0. Counter hypothesis falsified.
#   nothing else changed.
#
# key 66 latched 0 -> 1 on the first config change and has ignored four
# subsequent ones, including a full return to baseline. Sticky, not counting.
#
# Disabling sunrise/sunset was also invisible, matching enabling it.
